    <p class="mh">Guides</p><a class="mt" href="/nclex-study-guides"><b>Free study guides</b><small>Printable PDFs</small></a><a class="mt" href="/nclex-at-home"><b>NCLEX at home</b><small>What's coming</small></a><a class="mt" href="/nclex-prep-comparison"><b>Compare NCLEX prep</b><small>What each one includes</small></a><a class="mt" href="/about"><b>About Vexcura</b><small>Who built it</small></a>
    <p class="mh">Work with us</p><a class="mt" href="/careers"><b>Careers</b><small>Clinical education sales for RNs and LPNs</small></a>
    <p class="mh">Account</p><a class="mt" href="/#signup"><b>Join with a school code</b><small>Or request access</small></a><a class="mt" href="/#signin"><b>Sign in</b><small>Already have one</small></a>
   </div></details></nav></div></header><script>(function(){var d=document.querySelector(".mnav");if(!d)return;document.addEventListener("click",function(e){if(d.open&&!d.contains(e.target))d.open=false});document.addEventListener("keydown",function(e){if(e.key==="Escape"&&d.open){d.open=false;d.querySelector("summary").focus()}})})();</script><main><nav class="crumb w" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/nclex-practice-questions">Practice questions</a> › <span>Safety and Infection Prevention and Control</span></nav><section class="hero w"><h1>Safety and Infection Prevention and Control NCLEX Practice Questions</h1><p class="lead">Keeping clients and staff safe from harm and infection. Try 8 free questions below, each with the answer, a rationale, and the source it was checked against.</p>
      <p><span class="pill">10–16% of the NCLEX-RN</span><span class="pill">163 questions in Vexcura</span><span class="pill">Reviewed November 2026</span></p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/#practice/SIC">Practice more Safety and Infection Prevention and Control questions</a><a class="btn alt" href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Take the full practice exam</a></div></section>
      <div class="w"><h2>What this category covers</h2><p>Safety and Infection Prevention and Control is one of the eight client needs categories in the NCSBN NCLEX-RN test plan. Expect questions on Isolation precautions, PPE order, Fall prevention, Restraints, Emergency response, and Error prevention.</p>
      <div class="tips"><b>How to study Safety and Infection Prevention and Control</b><ul><li>Memorize isolation by organism: airborne (TB, measles, varicella), droplet (influenza, pertussis, mumps, meningococcus), contact (C. difficile, MRSA, scabies, RSV).</li><li>Drill the PPE sequences: on is gown, mask, eyewear, gloves; off is gloves, eyewear, gown, mask, then hand hygiene.</li><li>Review fall, seizure, suicide, and restraint safety; restraint is always the last option after alternatives are documented.</li><li>C. difficile and spores need soap and water. Alcohol rub does not kill spores.</li></ul></div>
      <h2>Free Safety and Infection Prevention and Control practice questions</h2><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="sic-002">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">After caring for a client with Clostridioides difficile, how should the nurse clean their hands?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-002"> <span><b>A.</b> Use alcohol rub before and after glove use</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-002"> <span><b>B.</b> Wash with soap and water after removing gloves</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-002"> <span><b>C.</b> Wear two pairs of gloves and skip hand hygiene</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-002"> <span><b>D.</b> Use an alcohol-based hand rub after removing gloves</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: B</p><p>C. difficile forms spores that alcohol does not kill. Soap and water with friction physically removes spores. Gloves never replace hand hygiene.</p><p class="src">Source: CDC. Hand Hygiene in Healthcare Settings; C. difficile clinical overview</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/caring-clostridioides-difficile-clean-hands-sic-002">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="sic-018">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Multiple choice · Analyze cues</div>
     <p class="stem">A client reports allergies to bananas, avocados, and kiwi. What should the nurse ask about next?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-018"> <span><b>A.</b> Iodine allergy</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-018"> <span><b>B.</b> Penicillin allergy</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-018"> <span><b>C.</b> Egg allergy</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-018"> <span><b>D.</b> Latex allergy</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: D</p><p>These foods cross-react with latex (latex-fruit syndrome). The nurse should screen for latex allergy and use latex-free equipment if present.</p><p class="src">Source: ACAAI. Latex Allergy (latex-fruit syndrome)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/reports-allergies-bananas-avocados-kiwi-ask-next-sic-018">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="sic-001">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">A client is admitted with suspected pulmonary tuberculosis. Which precautions should the nurse implement?</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-001"> <span><b>A.</b> Airborne precautions in a negative-pressure room with an N95 respirator</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-001"> <span><b>B.</b> Contact precautions with a gown and gloves</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-001"> <span><b>C.</b> Droplet precautions with a surgical mask</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-001"> <span><b>D.</b> Standard precautions only until sputum results return</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: A</p><p>TB spreads by airborne droplet nuclei. Clients need an airborne infection isolation (negative-pressure) room, and staff wear a fit-tested N95 or higher respirator. Precautions start on suspicion, not after confirmation.</p><p class="src">Source: CDC. 2007 Guideline for Isolation Precautions; CDC TB Infection Control (2005)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/admitted-suspected-pulmonary-tuberculosis-precautions-implement-sic-001">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="sata" id="sic-019">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Select all that apply · Generate solutions</div>
     <p class="stem">A client has MRSA in a wound. Which actions are part of contact precautions? Select all that apply.</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-sic-019"> <span><b>A.</b> Keep the client in a negative-pressure room</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-sic-019"> <span><b>B.</b> Wear an N95 respirator for all care</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-sic-019"> <span><b>C.</b> Place the client in a private room or cohort with another MRSA client</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-sic-019"> <span><b>D.</b> Wear a gown and gloves when entering the room</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="checkbox" name="q-sic-019"> <span><b>E.</b> Use dedicated equipment such as a stethoscope</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answers: C, D, E</p><p>Contact precautions require gown and gloves, dedicated or disinfected equipment, and a private room or cohorting. N95 respirators and negative pressure are airborne precautions.</p><p class="src">Source: CDC. 2007 Guideline for Isolation Precautions (Contact Precautions)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/mrsa-wound-actions-part-contact-precautions-select-sic-019">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="sic-011">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Multiple choice · Evaluate outcomes</div>
     <p class="stem">Which action breaks sterile technique?</p><ol><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-011"> <span><b>A.</b> Holding sterile gloved hands below the waist</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-011"> <span><b>B.</b> Facing the sterile field at all times</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-011"> <span><b>C.</b> Keeping a 1-inch (2.5 cm) border around the sterile field as nonsterile</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-011"> <span><b>D.</b> Pouring sterile solution without touching the container to the field</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: A</p><p>Anything below the waist or out of sight is considered contaminated. The outer inch of the field is nonsterile, the nurse should always face the field, and pouring without contact keeps the field sterile.</p><p class="src">Source: Potter &amp; Perry. Fundamentals of Nursing, 12th ed. (surgical asepsis)</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/action-breaks-sterile-technique-sic-011">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="sic-013">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">A nurse gets a needlestick injury from a used needle. What should the nurse do first?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-013"> <span><b>A.</b> Squeeze the site to make it bleed</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-013"> <span><b>B.</b> Wash the site with soap and water</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-013"> <span><b>C.</b> Report to employee health for testing</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-013"> <span><b>D.</b> Apply bleach to the puncture site</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: B</p><p>Immediately wash with soap and water, then report so post-exposure evaluation and prophylaxis can start, ideally within hours. Squeezing and caustic agents are not recommended.</p><p class="src">Source: CDC. Exposure to Blood: What Healthcare Personnel Need to Know</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/gets-needlestick-injury-used-needle-sic-013">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="sic-008">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Multiple choice · Generate solutions</div>
     <p class="stem">Which intervention is most appropriate for a client at high risk for falls?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-008"> <span><b>A.</b> Place the call light on the client's weaker side</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-008"> <span><b>B.</b> Keep the bed in the lowest position with the wheels locked</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-008"> <span><b>C.</b> Keep the room dark to promote sleep</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-008"> <span><b>D.</b> Raise all four side rails at night</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: B</p><p>A low, locked bed reduces injury if the client gets up or falls. Four raised side rails are a restraint and increase injury from climbing over. Rooms should have a night light, and the call light should be within reach on the stronger side.</p><p class="src">Source: AHRQ. Preventing Falls in Hospitals Toolkit</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/intervention-appropriate-high-risk-falls-sic-008">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article><article class="q" data-t="mc" id="sic-012">
     <div class="meta">Safety and Infection Prevention and Control · Multiple choice · Take action</div>
     <p class="stem">After giving a subcutaneous injection, what should the nurse do with the needle?</p><ol><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-012"> <span><b>A.</b> Bend the needle before disposal</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-012"> <span><b>B.</b> Place it on the bedside table to dispose of later</span></label></li><li data-c="1"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-012"> <span><b>C.</b> Activate the safety device and place it in a sharps container right away</span></label></li><li data-c="0"><label><input type="radio" name="q-sic-012"> <span><b>D.</b> Recap the needle using both hands</span></label></li></ol>
     <button class="btn chk" type="button">Check answer</button><p class="res" aria-live="polite"></p>
     <details><summary>Show answer and rationale</summary><p class="ans">Correct answer: C</p><p>Needles are not recapped by hand, bent, or broken. Use the safety device and discard immediately in a puncture-resistant sharps container at the point of use.</p><p class="src">Source: OSHA. Bloodborne Pathogens Standard, 29 CFR 1910.1030</p></details>
     <p class="more"><a href="/nclex-questions/giving-subcutaneous-injection-needle-sic-012">Open this question on its own page</a></p></article>
      <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/#practice/SIC">Practice more Safety and Infection Prevention and Control questions</a><a class="btn alt" href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Take the full practice exam</a></div><div class="split rev"><div class="st"><h2>See your Safety and Infection Prevention and Control score after a full exam</h2><p>The full adaptive practice exam runs 85 to 150 items on a five-hour clock and stops by the same rules as the NCLEX. Results break down by all 8 client needs categories, with a pass-likelihood estimate, so students know exactly what to study next. It's free for students through a licensed nursing school, and every plan includes everything.</p><p><a href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">The full practice exam</a></p></div><figure class="shot"><div class="dv dv-b"><div class="dv-bar" aria-hidden="true"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span><svg viewBox="0 0 12 14" width="9" height="11" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M3 6V4.2a3 3 0 0 1 6 0V6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"/><rect x="1.5" y="6" width="9" height="7" rx="1.6" fill="currentColor"/></svg>nclexsim.com</span></div><div class="dv-v"><img src="/img/exam-results-cats-1000.webp" srcset="/img/exam-results-cats-1600.webp 1600w, /img/exam-results-cats-1000.webp 1000w" sizes="(max-width: 760px) calc(100vw - 32px), 720px" width="1000" height="625" alt="Full practice exam results by NCLEX client needs category, from below to above the passing standard" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div></div><figcaption>Full-exam results by client needs category. Demo student.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="font-size:15.5px;color:var(--muted)">Took an outside NCLEX-readiness test at school? <a href="/ati-score-report-study-plan">Turn your score report into a study plan</a> built from the topics it lists.</p><h2>Other NCLEX categories</h2><div class="grid"><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/management-of-care"><b>Management of Care</b><small>15–21% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/health-promotion-and-maintenance"><b>Health Promotion and Maintenance</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/psychosocial-integrity"><b>Psychosocial Integrity</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/basic-care-and-comfort"><b>Basic Care and Comfort</b><small>6–12% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/pharmacology"><b>Pharmacological and Parenteral Therapies</b><small>13–19% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/reduction-of-risk-potential"><b>Reduction of Risk Potential</b><small>9–15% of the exam</small></a><a class="card" href="/nclex-practice-questions/physiological-adaptation"><b>Physiological Adaptation</b><small>11–17% of the exam</small></a></div><h2>Related</h2><ul class="rel"><li><a href="/free-nclex-practice-exam">Full adaptive NCLEX-RN practice exam</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-prioritization-questions">NCLEX prioritization questions</a></li><li><a href="/next-generation-nclex">Next Generation NCLEX (NGN) guide</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-study-guides">Free NCLEX study guides (PDF)</a></li><li><a href="/nclex-pass-guarantee">NCLEX pass guarantee: registration fee back if you qualify and don't pass</a></li></ul></div><section class="band vxs-band"><div class="vxs-bg" data-vxs-i="0" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="vxs-sh" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="w"><h2>Bring Vexcura to your nursing program</h2><p>1,220 cited NCLEX-RN questions, adaptive practice exams, 8 NGN case studies, an Epic-style academic EHR and a clinical instructor workspace. The school pays; students and instructors are always free, and every plan includes everything. Students: ask your instructor, or join with your school's code.</p><div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="/for-nursing-schools">See Vexcura for nursing schools</a><a class="btn alt" href="/for-nursing-schools#pricing">Pricing</a><a class="btn alt" href="/#signup">Join with a school code</a></div></div></section></main><footer class="foot mktf"><div class="w">
